+## Configuring the app
+
+A deployed app is configured through its env vars and the files beside
+`app.py`. A [deployment overlay](#deployment-overlay) supplies both, so nothing
+here needs an edit to a tracked file. An overlay for an app with managed
+sandboxes, the per-user Databricks connection and an MCP registry:
+
+```yaml
+# deploy/databricks/deploy.local.yml
+host: https://<workspace-host>
+extras: [kms]
+env:
+  OMNIGENT_CONFIG: server-config.yml
+  OMNIGENT_MCP_REGISTRY: mcp-registry.yml
+  OMNIGENT_DATABRICKS_CLIENT_ID: <oauth-client-id>
+  OMNIGENT_DATABRICKS_REDIRECT_URI: https://<app-url>/v1/connections/databricks/callback
+  OMNIGENT_CREDENTIAL_KMS_KEY_ID: <kms-key-arn>
+  OMNIGENT_CREDENTIAL_KMS_UC_SERVICE_CREDENTIAL: <service-credential-name>
+  AWS_DEFAULT_REGION: <aws-region>
+  OMNIGENT_SHARING_MODE: read_only
+  OMNIGENT_PUBLIC_SHARING: "off"
+secret_env:
+  OMNIGENT_DATABRICKS_CLIENT_SECRET:
+    scope: <secret-scope>
+    key: <secret-key>
+files:
+  server-config.yml: server-config.local.yml
+  mcp-registry.yml: mcp-registry.local.yml
+```
+
+### Server config
+
+`OMNIGENT_CONFIG` names a server config YAML; ship the file with the overlay's
+`files` and set the variable to its name. A relative path resolves against the
+directory the app is started in, the one `python app.py` runs from.
+
+A missing, unreadable or malformed file does not stop the app: it logs
+`server config <path> unreadable/invalid: … — falling back to env` and runs
+with defaults. After a deploy, look for `loaded server config from <path>` in
+the app's logs.
+
+The Apps entry point honours these keys:
+
+| Key | Use |
+|---|---|
+| `sandbox` | Managed sandbox hosts ([below](#server-managed-databricks-sandboxes)). A malformed section fails startup. |
+| `llm` | The server-side model, used by policies and by the built-in routing judge. |
+| `routing` | [Smart routing](#smart-routing). |
+| `policies`, `policy_modules` | Default policies and the modules that provide policy code. |
+| `execution_timeout` | Maximum wall-clock seconds for an agent loop (default 7200). |
+| `native_hook_allow_cache_seconds` | Seconds a native policy hook's allow decision is cached; unset keeps every hook a server round trip. |
+| `branding`, `default_agent_name`, `restrict_to_agent_names`, `session_title_instructions` | The web UI's branding, the New Chat picker and session titles. |
+| `automation_trigger_poll_minutes`, `scheduled_task_scheduler`, `recovery_prompt`, `filesystem_attachment_allowed_extensions`, `debug_router_modules` | Passed to the server as for `omnigent server`. |
+
+Not honoured here, although the Docker entry point reads them from the same
+file: `admins` and `allowed_domains` (the Apps entry point does not pass them
+to the server), and `database_uri`, `artifact_location`, `artifact_store_uri`,
+`host` and `port` (an app takes those from its Lakebase and volume resources
+and from `DATABRICKS_APP_PORT`).
+
+### Smart routing
+
+Smart routing is off on an app until the server config says how to route.
+Without a `routing:` block, the server builds the workspace router only when
+it finds a `kind: databricks` provider with a CLI `profile`, which an app does
+not have; and it builds the built-in judge only from an `llm:` block. With
+neither, `GET /v1/info` reports `smart_routing_enabled: false`.
+
+To route through the workspace's Unity Gateway:
+
+```yaml
+routing:
+  provider: external
+  base_url: https://<workspace-host>/ai-gateway/routing/v1
+  router_name: task_v1
+```
+
+- **Who calls the router.** With no `api_key` or `profile` in the block, the
+  server authenticates with the ambient Databricks SDK credentials, which on an
+  app are the app's service principal, not the user. It attaches them only
+  when the router's host is the ambient workspace host, so a `base_url`
+  elsewhere gets no workspace token.
+- **What is sent.** Each routing request carries up to the first 4,000
+  characters of the prompt and the candidate models.
+- **The router's own model.** The router makes its own model call;
+  `routing.selection_model` pins the model it uses, and `routing.menus`
+  replaces the candidate menu the router requires.
+- **The built-in judge.** An `llm:` block adds a second router, which serves
+  harnesses whose inference does not run through the workspace gateway.
+  `GET /v1/info` reports which of the two can answer in
+  `smart_routing_sources` (`external` and `oss`).
+- **The Smart Routing choice on the New Chat page** needs the external router,
+  both native wrapper agents (Claude and Codex) registered, both CLIs ready on
+  the selected host, and both running through the workspace gateway.
+- **Off.** `routing: {provider: none}` builds neither router.
+
+Not verified here: that an app's service principal is allowed to call the
+routing API in a given workspace, and a routed turn end to end.
+
+### MCP registry
+
+`OMNIGENT_MCP_REGISTRY` names the YAML catalog of MCP services the server
+offers; there is no other way to supply it. Ship it with the overlay's `files`.
+The format is in
+[`examples/mcp-registry/README.md`](../../examples/mcp-registry/README.md#administrator-configuration).
+An invalid catalog fails startup.
+
+- `auth: databricks` calls the service with the user's own Databricks account
+  connection, so it needs that connection configured (below).
+- `tools` is required and is an allowlist of exact tool names, unless the
+  registry in your build supports patterns (the format reference above says
+  so if it does). With exact names, generate it from each endpoint's tool list
+  and regenerate it when a service changes.
+
+Reported by one deployment and not verifiable from this repository: managed MCP
+URLs are `/api/2.0/mcp/functions/<catalog>/<schema>` for Unity Catalog
+functions and `/ai-gateway/mcp-services/<catalog>.<schema>.<name>` for Unity
+Gateway MCP services.
+
+### Sharing
+
+Two env vars set the sharing defaults, and neither is specific to Databricks:
+
+- `OMNIGENT_SHARING_MODE`: `on` (default), `read_only`,
+  `restricted_read_only` or `off`. It limits the grants people can create on
+  their sessions.
+- `OMNIGENT_PUBLIC_SHARING`: `off` stops anyone-with-the-link access; the
+  default is on.
+
+A session's agent runs as the session's owner, and with `proxy_bearer` the
+sandbox holds the owner's Databricks token. For a multi-user deployment, start
+from `read_only` and public sharing off unless letting collaborators drive each
+other's sessions is a deliberate choice.
+
+### Sessions need a host
+
+The app's entry point starts the server only; sessions run on hosts. Either
+configure managed sandboxes (below), or have each user attach a machine with
+`omnigent host https://<app-url>`.
+
+### Telemetry
+
+The `prod` target runs the app under `opentelemetry-instrument` and has the
+platform write logs, metrics and spans to `<schema>.otel_logs`,
+`<schema>.otel_metrics` and `<schema>.otel_spans`; `--otel-table-schema`
+chooses the schema (default `main.omnigent_logs`). The export API rejects
+tables in default storage, so the workspace needs a custom storage location;
+otherwise deploy with `--no-otel`. What the spans carry, and the variables that
+add content or user attribution, are in
+[`docs/databricks.md`](../../docs/databricks.md#4-mlflow-tracing-in-unity-catalog).
+
+Reported by one deployment and not verified here: a catalog bound read-only to
+the workspace also fails the export, so pick a schema the workspace can write.
+
+### Deploy notes
+
+- **uv.** The repo's `uv.toml` requires uv 0.13.4 or newer.
+- **`--allow-dirty`.** The deploy refuses to run unless the tree is clean and
+  `HEAD` is `origin/main`. Deploying a release tag or a branch therefore needs
+  `--allow-dirty`, and so does a tree that still holds the untracked wheels a
+  previous deploy left in `src/`. The overlay and its `*.local.yml` files are
+  gitignored and do not count.
+- **Wheels.** `deploy.py` builds its own wheels with the web UI kept outside
+  them. Wheels built with the web UI inside, as for other install paths, are
+  over the 10 MB per-file cap and cannot be reused with `--skip-build`.
+- **`--compute-size`.** `deploy.py` resizes an existing app before
+  `bundle deploy`, and the bundle itself does not declare a size. One
+  deployment reports that the resize is logged on every deploy and the app
+  stays at its previous size. Not verified or fixed here; check the app's size
+  in the workspace after a deploy.
+
